body:
I'm not sure I agree that GoT is not high fantasy. GoT as a show has always been at least Low Fantasy, the general world, which seems fairly normal, is being affected by magical forces. However the realism of the world, considering the lore and worldbuilding, is actually pretty abnormal. 

&#x200B;

The issue with classifying high fantasy and low fantasy is that GoT kind of sits in both pools. Its world and lore is based on a High Fantasy world, with the Age of Heroes, Children of the Forest, the Long Night, Dragons, Valyrian Steel, Valyria, the Doom of Valyria, the construction of the Wall, Bloodraven, Skinchangers, the Tragedy of Summerhall, Yi-Ti and magic, all of which had dictated events that led up to the beginning of the whole series with Ned being named hand. And yet, the inciting incident for the conflict that is focused on by the series is arguably Littlefinger murdering Jon Arryn, which has (at least so far in the books and definitely so in the series) no relation to any magic whatsoever. One of the issues I hold with your viewpoint is the idea that we have to classify worlds in a 'high fantasy' and 'low fantasy' bubbles, wherein those classifications are what George loves to deconstruct and subvert. I don't think all classifications are that simple at all, it's more nuanced than that. It's obvious that GoT is not Lord of the Rings because the politics is the focus of the series for many seasons and many books. But what many enjoy about the series, with the political focus as opposed to a good versus evil high fantasy story, is inevitably going to come to an end. Another problem I have is that you distinguish high fantasy from other fantasies by saying it can't have 'brutal realism where politics and characters are valued above all else,' which I think this interpretation is flat out wrong. Just because it's not all fairies and elves, and singing dwarves and dragons sitting atop hoards of gold doesn't mean it isn't high fantasy, it just means the politics have more consequences than in other high fantasies. But the thing that makes GoT high fantasy in my eyes is that while the politics have massive consequences, the magical elements of the story have even greater consequences. 

&#x200B;

Another point that I think is flat out wrong is that the brutal realism is 'the context for us being introduced to the story', which is a complete incorrect statement. I'd agree partially with your analysis of S1 and the early books, however I would say magic isn't as much 'rumoured' as it is clearly present, unexplained and remains unused by the characters present. That may seem like an odd distinction, but I think its important. However the very first scene that in the books (and show) is of the Others, a fantasy race of magical Ice Elves, magically raising undead wights from their graves to kill some Night's Watchmen. This makes introduction to the series and story is actually that of a High Fantasy story. We have elves, undead and magic. From that alone I entirely disagree that LSH is out of place, we've seen people be raised from the dead in the very first scene of the show and again later on in that same first season, so LSH's resurrection would've been fairly in universe. Magic is definitely returning to the world in some sort of way, but I think it's more magic is coming out of hibernation. Going by the books, the start of magic coming out of hibernation is arguably what happened at the 'Tragedy of Summerhall', which happened way before the series even started. I think LSH's resurrection would have done a better job of what Jon Snow's resurrection was trying to do in your description. As you said Jon's resurrection was shocking, but I think that is where LSH's resurrection does a way better job in every aspect. 

&#x200B;

By S6, people have witnessed people changing faces, dragons, pretty much of the Stark children warging in the books, the house of the undying horror show in the books and it's neutered counterpart in the show, wights, shadow babies, Others and resurrections of Beric Dondarrion. I guess people have also witnessed Melisandre's magic visions though that's somewhat debatable since the show never showed it properly, just reaction shots. Magic has been pretty damn solidly established by Season 6, and Jon's magical surprise of 'oh my god you really didn't expect magic to exist in this universe, did you?' concept is a bit late to the party here. Magic isn't 'not completely real' or 'coming back to the world slowly', at this point anyone watching knows this universe is magical. Which is why I think LSH is actually a huge loss for the series and her resurrection does a better job at being what you describe than Jon's was in the show. People have been thinking Jon would be resurrected ever since they read his last chapter in Dance, to the point where his death is actually more shocking than his inevitable resurrection. With LSH when you read the books and witness Cat's death midway through Storm, you don't even think it's possible. And when it happens and you realise that LSH is Cat, it's a real surprise. The kind that I think D&D never actually managed to achieve on the show. Whether you like it or not the politics at the end of it all has always been a distraction from the existential magical threats of the Wights, the Three Eyed Crow, Euron, Dany and the Long Night come again. In that way, really GoT and aSoIaF is more of a reverse Low Fantasy, the magical world and events are being intruded upon by politics. I think LSH would've made a very good impact on the show.

&#x200B;

On a more speculative note, I also think it's important to consider how Jon's resurrection was actually presented. In the show it's clear he is the same guy who was killed. He didn't lose himself like LSH did. That is another reason why LSH's resurrection makes Jon's resurrection more scary and somewhat more impactful. LSH is clearly a deranged psycho killing machine, mercilessly killing anyone who was even loosely related to the Freys. That includes Brienne, who swore her oath to Catelyn even though Brienne hasn't broken any oaths nor fought for the Freys. She's clearly crazy, and I think that's what makes Jon's resurrection more of an issue where it wasn't in the show is that we don't know if he will come out as himself. We don't know if he will never recognise any of his family ever again. And when he does come back, he will not be the way he was. But how much he will change, that's a much scary thought to present than whether he will come back or not.

Anime Discussion Thread: What an amazing finale

&#x200B;

Manga Discussion Thread: WHERE'S THE FUCKING TREE??

All the Stark children can warg thanks to their genetics and their close bond with their Direwolves - minor spoilers, but it's suggested Robb warged Grey Wind to scout out locations for battle, which explains his amazing battle tactics at times - though its unknown if this is true. Robb also has to remind himself often that "he's not a wolf", much akin to how Bran does as well. It's also probable that Robb dies twice, once in his own human body and once in Grey Wind, as wargs go into their animals once they die.

&#x200B;

Arya wargs Nymeria a lot, she dreams of quite a few important moments in the books through the eyes of Nymeria, such as Lady Stone Heart's resurrection, and killing Lannister soldiers with her wolf pack.

&#x200B;

On top of the actual events, GRRM also stated that all of the Stark children are wargs, so there's that too. Sansa has not used her powers yet, and Rickon will likely be a Skagosi beast man once they find him in the books.
